# User defined shot attributes, issue 571.
#
# `shotattr' works like `flags': it sets state that applies to the shots that
# follow, until it is changed or the centreline ends. Attributes are exported
# one row per attribute in the SHOT_ATTRIBUTE table.
#
# Three things must be told apart:
#   shotattr <name> <value>   sets the attribute
#   shotattr <name> ""        removes it, like the station `attr' flag
#   shotattr not <name>       removes it as well
# A value of `false' is a value, not a removal: recording that a passage is
# not fossil must stay different from not recording anything about it.

survey shotattr

  centreline
    data normal from to length compass clino

    # no attribute yet
    1 2 10 0 0

    shotattr fossil true
    2 3 10 0 0

    # two attributes at once; the export order follows the attribute name
    shotattr phreatic "large gallery"
    3 4 10 0 0

    # `false' is stored, it does not remove the attribute
    shotattr fossil false
    4 5 10 0 0

    # `not' removes it
    shotattr not fossil
    5 6 10 0 0

    # an empty value removes it too
    shotattr phreatic ""
    6 7 10 0 0

    # a value needing SQL escaping
    shotattr comment "l'entree, dite ""la chatiere"""
    7 8 10 0 0

    # a group inherits the state it was entered with, and what the group sets
    # is dropped again on endgroup. The parent attribute must be set *before*
    # the group, otherwise the test only proves that nothing leaks out of it.
    shotattr not comment
    shotattr context outer
    8 9 10 0 0
    group
      # inherited: context only
      9 10 10 0 0
      shotattr digging true
      # inherited plus the group's own
      10 11 10 0 0
    endgroup
    # restored: context again, digging gone
    11 12 10 0 0

  endcentreline

  # Interleaved data build legs through a different path. The state is captured
  # when the station line creates the leg, so `shotattr' written after station
  # 13 lands on the 14--15 shot rather than on 13--14. That is exactly what
  # `flags' does in interleaved data; the test pins the consistency, not an
  # opinion about which shot ought to carry it.
  centreline
    data normal station newline length compass clino

    12
    10 0 0
    13
    shotattr fossil true
    10 0 0
    14
    shotattr not fossil
    10 0 0
    15

  endcentreline

  # Two attribute names longer than ten characters, whose first ten characters
  # differ. They stay distinct in SHOT_ATTRIBUTE but collide in the DBF, where
  # both become `abcdefghi_': expdbf_field_name builds an eleven character
  # disambiguated name and shapelib then writes only ten of them.
  #
  # shots3d.dbf.ref pins that behaviour on purpose. When expdbf_field_name is
  # fixed -- a separate change, since it renames existing fields -- this
  # reference has to be updated, which is how the fix will be noticed here.
  centreline
    data normal from to length compass clino

    shotattr abcdefghiX12 left
    shotattr abcdefghiY34 right
    15 16 10 0 0

  endcentreline

endsurvey
